    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $e: X \rightarrow Y$ be an arbitrary section of $p$, and choose a Cartesian section $e': X' \rightarrow Y$ with the same target. Since $e'$ is Cartesian, there exists a diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { X \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  Y \ar [d]^{\id _{Y}} \\ X' \ar [r] &amp;  Y } \]
</div>
    <p>
       in $\calM $, which we may regard as a morphism $\phi $ from $e \in \calC $ to $e' \in \calC '$. In view of Proposition <a href="/tag/A4E3E0" data-tag="A4E3E0">5.2.7.8</a>, it will suffice to show that $\phi $ exhibits $e'$ as a $\calC '$-localization of $\calC $. In other words, we must show that for <em>any</em> Cartesian section $e'': X'' \rightarrow Y''$, composition with $\phi $ induces a homotopy equivalence $\bHom _{\calC }( e', e'') \rightarrow \bHom _{\calC }(e,e'').$ This follows immediately from Lemma <a href="/tag/9F4DEB" data-tag="9F4DEB">5.5.3.15</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
